<p>The best tool is the one you stop noticing. Not because it is unimportant, but because it has done its job so completely that your attention is free to move on to the work itself.</p>

<p>This is harder than it sounds. Software has a gravity to it. Every feature wants to announce itself. Every setting wants to be adjusted. Every update wants a tour. We build products that compete for the very attention they were supposed to return to us.</p>

<h2>Invisible by design</h2>

<p>A good hammer does not ask how you’re feeling. A good pencil does not sync. A good notebook does not remind you that it has been three days since your last entry. These tools have opinions, but they keep them quiet.</p>

<ul>
  <li>It remembers what matters, so I don’t have to.</li>
  <li>It stays close without getting in the way.</li>
  <li>It lets me leave without making me feel guilty.</li>
</ul>

<p>That last point is underrated. The best software is not the kind you cannot put down. It is the kind you can put down because you trust it to be there when you come back.</p>

<p>I’ve been trying to remove things from my own work instead of adding them. Fewer buttons. Fewer choices. Fewer moments where the interface says look at me. The goal is not emptiness. The goal is the quiet that makes room for the work.</p>
